PASS level_intents: all 120 levels have a specific intent
PASS no_filler_levels: no trivial filler levels after the tutorial
FAIL level_design: level-design review 6.5/10 (bar 8), 4 level(s) below 6/10: A coherent, well-structured and fair-minded set. Every level has a stated purpose and a labelled trap, and chapters introduce mechanics alone before combining them. It falls short of top-tier because the core insight is nearly always "shorten the busy line, put the scarce resource on the bottleneck." Boards are small and look alike, many late levels reuse the same plus/T/ring skeletons, and a few breathers and send-offs are not actually easier than their neighbours. Several levels also have misleading or inconsistent intent, and a few have a single early fatal choice (the card-pick levels). Below are the levels I rate under 8. Levels not listed rate 8 or higher. Curve: Strong, disciplined structure: 120 levels in about 12 themed chapters, each introducing one mechanic alone (tunnel, carriage, hill, ferry, snow, market, interchange, loop, wave, heritage). Each chapter then moves practise, twist, combine, boss, breather, mastery. Every level has a stated trap and an aha. The problems are:
1) Aha monotony. Nearly every level is "split the line, keep it short, put the carriage or tunnel on the busy line." Late chapters repeat the same capacity arithmetic (hops x seats) in new costumes. Players will learn the heuristic "shorter lines" and stop thinking.
2) Boss and finale pressure is high. Many casual rates fall at or below 50% (L83 43%, L100 49%, L102 47%, L119 47%, L107 48%) against a ≤65% last-third average. Several skilled rates sit at 81-83%, which is fine. The casual average over the last third looks near 55-60%, which is on target. Some isolated spikes are still too sharp.
3) Several breathers are not easier than their neighbours (L112 50%, L116 51%, L103 80% but the intent is thin). L84, a "gentle send-off," has casual 73% and skilled 76%, so the gap is tiny and the level is unreadable.
4) Many boards are tiny 4-7 node graphs with the same plus/T/ring skeletons, so there is little spatial variety. Boards are sparse, with no scenic or geographic identity.
5) Opening is gentle and well paced for L1-L4. L1-L3 meet ≥80%. There are a few too many easy levels around L19, L25, L31 and L1-L4. The mid game has no real run of difficulty ramps. The cap on easy streaks holds.
6) Heavy reliance on "trap" levels where the wrong first move is fatal and only shows up later. The forecast mitigates this, but the "single hidden fatal move early" concern is real in the card levels (L16, L32, L58, L78, L96).
7) Order issue: L13-L24 tunnels reuse the L3/L5 split lesson with the tunnel as a constraint. Ferry L49-L60 and snow L61-L72 both reuse "the shared bottleneck line should be shorter." The loop city is the freshest.
Overall the set is solid and well built but too formulaic to match Royal Match or Monument Valley's variety.
REDESIGN THESE LEVELS (keep the ones not listed):
L83 (5/10): Casual 43% is an isolated spike below the neighbours; the right choice depends on timing of markets across the 7 weeks; the aha is a timing riddle with a hidden fatal pick. → Show the market weeks on a calendar strip and lower the goal.
L84 (5/10): Casual 73% versus skilled 76% with 11 start choices: unreadable and effectively luck; a 'gentle send-off' with a 3rd star that is very hard. → Reduce start choices to about 6 and give a clear preview so skilled players reach about 95%.
L112 (5/10): A 'breather' with a 50% casual win rate and a trap that loses by week 2; it's not a breather. → Lower the goal, make it a real breather, or relabel it a combine level.
L116 (5/10): Casual 51%, a hard level in a late chapter; the decoy rung is a hidden trap. → Show the loop-closing hint.
L11 (6/10): Optimal moves unknown (search cap) with a card safety net. The finale is the same lesson as L6 on a larger board, and the card makes the solution muddy. → Remove the card. Make the late station I the only novelty and cut the board to 8 stops.
L12 (6/10): Recap of lessons with 11 start choices. Casual 89% is easy for a mastery level. → Raise the goal so the shared-stop idea is required, not optional (two tidy lines should fail the 3rd star clearly and the base goal narrowly).
L16 (6/10): Fatal card pick in week 3 is hidden; casual guessing. Reads as a trap rather than a test. → Show the forecast change when previewing each card so the right pick is deducible.
L19 (6/10): Casual 93% and skilled 100% is fine for a breather, but it adds nothing new. → Add a scenic secret (hidden shortcut) as the star puzzle.
L23 (6/10): Casual 66% is fine, but the board is thin for a boss and the idea repeats L22. → Add a third bank or land route so there is a real alternative.
L27 (6/10): Counter-intuitive twist, but it relies on hidden arithmetic about transfers and the board has empty arms with long corridors. → Shorten the arms. Show a transfer-load preview.
L28 (6/10): Casual 74%. The goal (166 of 210) is hard to read. → Display the cup riders' route on the preview.
L30 (6/10): Casual 69%. The solution requires 10 moves in week 1 over three weeks, and the two-tunnel/two-carriage plan is long to read. → Reduce to one carriage and one late ground.
L31 (6/10): A kite shape with two easy solutions is pleasant but the one-line star duplicates L7. → Choose a different star condition, such as 'no queue above 6'.
L32 (6/10): Hidden fatal pick (carriage vs tracks) with casual 73% and skilled 86%. Guessing. → Show the preview of tracks needed on the card.
L33 (6/10): Two long empty corridors (six rows) waste the board. → Compress the lane to one hop with a 'long' icon.
L34 (6/10): Casual 70%, skilled 89%. The path through the tunnel versus the long road is confusing. → Shorten the C-D-F-E road and show hop counts.
L35 (6/10): Search cap. Casual 68%. The rule 'end both lines at the ground' repeats L22. → Add a ring option and an exact sequence for the carriages.
L36 (6/10): Intent text is contradictory on carriage placement and the board is dense with text. → Simplify to one late crowd and one carriage.
L38 (6/10): Practises the same 'put the long thing on the quiet line' idea again. → Add a second hill that tempts differently.
L40 (6/10): Hills plus carriage is a repeat of L26/L33. The unused D and F stops are clutter. → Remove D and F or give them a role.
L41 (6/10): Casual 75%, skilled 89%. The aha repeats L39 (the flat road beats the climb). → Replace with a level where a hill decides which Key is nearest, or merge with L45.
L42 (6/10): Casual 67%, skilled 87%. Another carriage-on-the-busy-line riddle, with unstated loads. → Show the line load meter.
L43 (6/10): Fine but near-duplicate of L31/L19. → Add a unique cozy feature, such as a hidden extra station.
L44 (6/10): Another late-station-next-to-end level, a repeat of L18/L22/L24. → Replace the late-station idea with a hill-driven nearest-Key puzzle.
L46 (6/10): A card level with a hidden fatal pick: casual 67%. → Preview card effect on the forecast.

level-design score 6.5/10 — A coherent, well-structured and fair-minded set. Every level has a stated purpose and a labelled trap, and chapters introduce mechanics alone before combining them. It falls short of top-tier because the core insight is nearly always "shorten the busy line, put the scarce resource on the bottleneck." Boards are small and look alike, many late levels reuse the same plus/T/ring skeletons, and a few breathers and send-offs are not actually easier than their neighbours. Several levels also have misleading or inconsistent intent, and a few have a single early fatal choice (the card-pick levels). Below are the levels I rate under 8. Levels not listed rate 8 or higher.
curve: Strong, disciplined structure: 120 levels in about 12 themed chapters, each introducing one mechanic alone (tunnel, carriage, hill, ferry, snow, market, interchange, loop, wave, heritage). Each chapter then moves practise, twist, combine, boss, breather, mastery. Every level has a stated trap and an aha. The problems are:
1) Aha monotony. Nearly every level is "split the line, keep it short, put the carriage or tunnel on the busy line." Late chapters repeat the same capacity arithmetic (hops x seats) in new costumes. Players will learn the heuristic "shorter lines" and stop thinking.
2) Boss and finale pressure is high. Many casual rates fall at or below 50% (L83 43%, L100 49%, L102 47%, L119 47%, L107 48%) against a ≤65% last-third average. Several skilled rates sit at 81-83%, which is fine. The casual average over the last third looks near 55-60%, which is on target. Some isolated spikes are still too sharp.
3) Several breathers are not easier than their neighbours (L112 50%, L116 51%, L103 80% but the intent is thin). L84, a "gentle send-off," has casual 73% and skilled 76%, so the gap is tiny and the level is unreadable.
4) Many boards are tiny 4-7 node graphs with the same plus/T/ring skeletons, so there is little spatial variety. Boards are sparse, with no scenic or geographic identity.
5) Opening is gentle and well paced for L1-L4. L1-L3 meet ≥80%. There are a few too many easy levels around L19, L25, L31 and L1-L4. The mid game has no real run of difficulty ramps. The cap on easy streaks holds.
6) Heavy reliance on "trap" levels where the wrong first move is fatal and only shows up later. The forecast mitigates this, but the "single hidden fatal move early" concern is real in the card levels (L16, L32, L58, L78, L96).
7) Order issue: L13-L24 tunnels reuse the L3/L5 split lesson with the tunnel as a constraint. Ferry L49-L60 and snow L61-L72 both reuse "the shared bottleneck line should be shorter." The loop city is the freshest.
Overall the set is solid and well built but too formulaic to match Royal Match or Monument Valley's variety.
